  more recent issue right and that is  since the onset of the kovite 19  pandemic there has been an up rising of  xenophobia against the chinese community  and in a sense the broader asian  community and this has not only been uh  you know there's not just been an  increase in hate speech there have  actually been an increase in hate crimes  against asians as um you know  admitted by the fbi  so it's a pretty serious problem so it  makes sense then that uh the university  of georgia tech decided to come up with  a model that can detect  anti-asian covert 19 hate speech  so for this problem they define  anti-asian covert 19 hate speech as  antagonistic speech that has one or more  covet 19 key words is directed toward an  individual or group of asian people and  is abusive derogatory or assigns blame  for the creation and spread of poverty  so  let's move on and look at how they  proceeded about this  since this was a new problem to solve  right um the  researchers had to collect a data set  and they did that by looking at  um the twitter api again and they had a  list of keywords this time  and um they were able to  query three million tweets actually and  obviously that's too big so they ended  up randomly  sub sampling uh you know some part of  the data set and manually annotating  some part of that subset and then they  used a gender they generated a text  classifier to annotate the others this  is a common practice we see especially  when we are dealing with large scale  data sets because manual annotation  takes a lot of time and it's not simply  feasible most of the time  but  after they had their data set uh you  know they cleaned the text up and they  converted everything to lower case  and in this case what the researchers  did is that they decided to compare  glove embeddings and bottom embeddings  to see which one solves the problem  better and to give you a little bit of  context what word embeddings are in  general is basically a  vector representation of words with  context so what that means is that in a  vector space  words that have similar meaning will  have a similar representation and  they'll be closer to each other so in  this case you know maybe words that are  related to the 4-bit 19 pandemic are in  one cluster or you know they're closer  together in the vector space and um  words that are random like you know  maybe cat dog you know sky whatever  these words would be further apart so  there are different kinds of embeddings  generated by different models and gloves  and weddings and board embeddings are  two examples of this  and they found that bird embeddings suit  their problem statement better  so then they trained multiple supervised  algorithms again on these uh embeddings  and the data set that they have now  embedded dataset and they found that  although most of the classifiers right  like the logistic retesting knife base  random forest  all of them had almost the same  performance but they found that the  logistic regression classifier performs  a little bit better with an accu  accuracy of 0.86  and the metric they used is something  called auc roc which is basically a  graph that gives you area under the  curve of the receiver optimistic  characteristic  so what that means is that it basically  plots the true positive rate against the  false positive rate at  various  levels so it's a really accurate metric  and it's actually preferred over  accuracy and sometimes even over f score  actually depending on the problem  statement at hand  so once that's done  uh yeah i guess you can see now that you  know machine learning models are being  uh used very efficiently to solve this  problem of hate speech detection  and if you have any background in  machine learning already you know that  you know  uh  an  ensemble approach could go a long way  here so you might be wondering that okay  if individual classifiers are performing  so well  uh how well can an ensemble of these  classifiers perform  and this is actually a  question that motivated my own research  project  and currently it's under the paper is  under review but  yeah it turns out that ensembl models  can actually perform very well and  although our problem statement was a  little bit different initially like it  was more along the lines of cyber crime  prevention and threat detection  but uh definitely a part of our solution  proved that an ensemble of you know  knife based classifier support vector  machine random forest and decision tree  could actually outperform the  state-of-the-art architectures at sexism  detection and this includes the deep  learning architectures as well so we  achieved an accuracy of 97  on our hate speech detection data set  but  yeah i think one of the reasons this  works so well is that you know we were  able to leverage the benefits of the  wisdom of the crowd principle behind  ensemble learning uh combined with  algorithms that historically perform  well at text classification even though  they use you know diverse algorithms in  themselves so  yeah now  i guess all of this begs the question  why do we even need deep learning right  because if we're able to achieve um you  know  97 90 accuracy with just ensemble  learning and machine learning then do we  even need deep learning for this and the  short answer is yes because even in  general like in the field of ai deep  learning does a great job at solving  problems that are really really  complicated and you know have  constrained environments  uh in terms of the learning you know  conditions that are sort of in the  problem statement  so to give you some context of that  right let me talk about my first  publication  which was published at acl it's called  hold on honey men at work and uh we  basically wanted to detect sexism in  popular sitcoms  and we ended up using a semi supervised  approach for this and  it was actually a very complex problem  statement so let me give you some more  context of this we basically wanted to  build a model that can detect sexism and  dialogues of popular sitcoms  uh and  there were quite a few challenges for  this but the first one was that there  was very little data to work with in  that domain  right and  manual annotation is extremely time  consuming and sometimes it's not even  that efficient because of you know bias  that is bound to creep in  uh and then if we look at using another  domain which is what we ended up doing  anyway  the challenge was to you know being uh  challenges to being able to generalize  across  different domains then you know  the shows  even within sitcom to sitcom there's  going to be some linguistic difference  and then if you look for a domain that  actually has a lot of data uh you're  going to end up you know complicating it  linguistically it's going to be more  difficult to link have linguistic  generalization across domains so these  were some of the problems that we faced  and it turned out that the solution was  a combination of deep learning semi  supervised learning and transfer  learning  so i'm not going to get into uh  the methodology of my paper  for  you know in great detail for the  interest of time  but we did have three parts of our data  set and that did include tweets and the  reason we needed tweets was that we  needed to establish some sense of you  know  uh  training data for the baseline model  so we had tweets in there we took we  borrowed the tweets from benchmark hate  speech data set and we had manually  annotated dialogues and then we had a  combination of these two to generate  pseudo-labor dialogues so our final data  set consisted of three parts and even  then right it was a huge data set in the  ending but even then simple supervised  machine learning algorithms couldn't  give us an accuracy of 55 56  but on the other hand we used a  bi-directional lstm  uh on the baseline data set and we found  that the accuracy is 77  so you can see like you know how much of  an improvement there is and finally like  with the transfer learning and the  pseudo labeling techniques the  bile stem was able to achieve an  accuracy of around 80  so this is just going to show that you  know deep learning is necessary and it's  necessary  in places where the problem statement is  a lot more complex right  so i do have uh the link to the paper  here and you should definitely check it  out if this is something that interests  you because  this is a bit of a niche domain right i  mean we have a lot of work done on hp's  for social media but here's the thing  hate speech is not just on social media  it's everywhere right so this is one of  those papers that ventures to like be on  social media and still looks to detect  its speech so you should definitely  check it out  next i want to talk about something even  more interesting which is hate speech  detection in memes using something  called visual bird  so it's no secret then that today memes  are a very popular means of  communication in some cases even a  popular name for bonding  right and there was actually a challenge  called the hateful memes challenge where  there was you know the goal was to be  able to detect hate speech in memes  so obviously you can see that this is  one of those problems that combines  language and vision together right and  the challenge had hosted a name data set  of like 10 000 names that had to be  classified as either hateful or neutral  now this deviates a little bit from the  standard text classification task  because although it's hate speech  detection it's no longer just text  classification right uh if we are  looking to identify speech and names we  want to make sure that we're picking up  on visual cues and you know linguistic  cues so that's why obviously like you  can see how complicated a problem this  is and that's why visual board was  useful here which is a deep learning  architecture  so  you can see here like the lines that i'm  thinking along right like i want to be  able to show you that deep learning is  necessary for extremely complicated  problems  and so let's look into how this  approach was developed  uh firstly i want to point out the  difference between bird and visual world  and that difference is that the latter  is visually counted now what i mean by  that is that visual bird uses  transformers to jointly model words and  parts of images whereas like the  original board model was just meant to  you know model worlds  and even though visual world has  actually proved uh you know  highly successful at  many many tasks it's still a bit of a  question you know does it understand the  desired cross model representation  maybe maybe not you know  we have no way to say for sure so early  on in the research community  but  for this particular task right uh  the visual world model was  able to secure a score of 0.811  and it was the winning model so you can  see that deep learning is definitely  needed even in the hate speech detection  domain right and i'm not going into too  much depth and to explain how this  visual world model works or how you know  the methodology was devised because it's  a bit of a cross over between computer  vision and natural language processing  and  you know it will just take too long and  i've linked both of the papers below i  definitely think both of them are very  interesting and you know it's something  uh very different and something very  innovative so do check it out if you  have the time  uh one more thing that uh deep learning  and nlp are useful for  is the multilingual heat speech  detection  uh because the challenge in being able  to detect hate speech in multiple  languages is being able to use the same  architecture to understand and identify  hate's full speech in multiple languages  right and as you would probably guess if  you have a background in machine  learning is that  supervised learning algorithms are way  too simplistic for this and they have  not been successful at this task  and multilingual hate speech detection  is a very very popular research field in  its own right and as of today we have  models that can detect hate speech in  english spanish french arabic chinese  japanese and even code mixed languages  like hindi and english right so  there are a lot of successful  publications in this realm i do urge you  to check it out if you're interested in  this because  finally when we talk about natural  language processing right english is not  just the only natural language out there  right we want  computers to be interfaced with us in  all language to be able to interface  with us in all possible languages right  so this is a very interesting subfield  within the hate speech detection domain  and  yeah i hope i was able to explain you  know  why deep learning is necessary in the  hate speech detection community despite  the  performance of machine learning  classifiers  and yeah so coming  forward and moving on to the next topic  which is when should we use pitch like  when is deep learning supposed to be  used when is machine learning supposed  to be used for these kind of problems  even in general there's no good answer  to this question right i mean there's no  firm answer at least it's not like  there's one specific use case that calls  for deep learning or one specific use  case that calls for machine learning  but there is definitely uh you know  a good rule of thumb that i like to use  which is that deep learning is usually a  good choice for complicated problems  that have a lot of constraints in the  learning environment so like we saw in  our paper uh papers that we just went  over right uh if you want to look for  something that's multilingual or  something that needs both vision and  language basically problems that are  just  more complicated than simple text  classification  deep learning is a good choice  but that being said i personally feel  that it's always good to try out some  supervisor unsupervised machine learning  algorithms at the beginning of any task  just to establish a baseline right  because once you're able to decide uh  you know how well can machine learning  classifiers do you can really quickly  tell whether the problem statement calls  for ensembl learning or whether it calls  for deep learning or you know basically  it just helps you plan out your next  steps in the modeling phase  so  yeah  let's move on to the last part of our  talk today which is um  open source data sets and libraries for  us to use  and in terms of uh  libraries we do have you know pytorch  tensorflow cameras python libraries like  scikit-learn nltk these are all open  source and if you've done any work in  the machine learning community you  already know this but what i thought it  is much more interesting to discuss is  that the data sets right um  we have something called papers with  code and i'm not sure how many of you  are aware of this  but the papers with code has 52 hate  speech detection data sets right so if  you go to that site and you just search  the term hate speech detection data sets  you get 52 results now these data sets  are for racism detection sexism  detection there are  english data sets there are chinese  datasets there are even arabic data sets  so there's a lot of data for you to  already get started with another thing  is that i have a data set hosted on my  github right and  and that is containing of sitcom  dialogues that are annotated for sexism  so if you feel like you want to venture  out into you know detecting sexism in  movies or in tv shows you should  definitely check that out as well  so yeah with that being said that's  about it thank you for being here i hope  you learned something by coming to this  talk and i hope i was able to motivate  you to look into hate speech detection  problems as budding researchers or as  budding ai practitioners thank you so  much
 

